== Positional edge-tuple repair

Round three closed the bounded captured *object-key signatures* but normalized
all array positions to a wildcard. For `payload.edges[i]`, that admitted either
the incidence or edge-data object at any position and admitted extra tuple
elements. A mirrored third edge-data object carrying an unchecked numerator, or
an extra incidence object, therefore passed semantics with unchanged numerator
FNV.

Round four adds one positional heterogeneous-tuple contract. Every captured
`payload.edges[i]` must be an array of exactly two elements. Position 0 must be
the supported incidence object with exactly `source` and `target`; position 1
must match one of the two supported edge-data signatures, differing only by the
legitimate optional `num_interface`. Array/object types and tuple length are
validated before owner construction.

The checker also traverses every payload key named `numerator`,
`numerator_prefactor`, `projector` or ending in `_interface`. Such a key is
admitted only at its exact aggregate, vertex or edge-data owner path. No
expression or interface can ride through another structurally valid branch.
After semantic checks, omission remains limited to those exact known owner
paths; every other field stays in the cross-route comparison.

This is not a claim that the entire JSON schema is typed or closed. Round four
closes the supported object-key signatures plus the positional two-element edge
tuple. Other array cardinalities and leaf types remain immutable-capture facts.
For example, mirrored changes of `aut` to an array, `symmetry_factor` to an
array, or `source_slot` from number to string are outside the semantic theorem
and are rejected only by frozen capture identity.

